<p>Hello there, Caspian2. Thank you, again, for reading my concept and giving me feedback.</p>
<p>From what I understand, you seem to approve SCP-XXXX's physical functions and the historical background. Your biggest concern, however, is understanding SCP-XXXX-1, imagining the object and its lack of tension. I will gladly discuss these elements.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;More&nbsp;detailed&nbsp;explanation</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>What exactly is SCP-xxxx-1? What exactly is this “non-physical entity right next to SCP-XXXX”?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I acknowledge your trouble with understanding SCP-XXXX-1, as the description was somewhat shallow. SCP-XXXX-1 is an invisible entity that contains the consciousness of the subject within SCP-XXXX. A ghost would be the most fitting description. I suppose what confuses you the most is <em>why</em> SCP-XXXX-1 is summoned right next to the capsule. No excuses here, it was just bad creativity. I only added that part because I thought it would confuse readers if I had not specified the location of SCP-XXXX-1's occurrence. I must rethink this occurrence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But I’m having trouble actually picturing the object.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That can only mean there's trouble with my wording. To sum it up, imagine a metallic cylinder big enough to contain a human inside of it, hence it is the &quot;sleeping capsule&quot;. The capsule has another metallic compound attached underneath it to ensure it doesn't roll sideways.<br />
<a href="https://cdnb.artstation.com/p/assets/images/images/015/666/997/large/zeeshan-mazhar-sleeping-pod.jpg?1549178959">Here's an image for inspiration.</a><br />
The image is obviously not the actual SCP, but to visualize the object's dimensions.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There isn’t much intrigue, tension or conflict present in the concept.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Agreed, it is pretty boring and opposes no threat whatsoever. Since this is my first concept, I wanted to start off small, since I read somewhere on this website that dangerous doesn't always make it interesting. Due to the limited quantity of words, I could not write the procedures. In fact, there is some danger/caution when using this capsule. As mentioned, the gas will continuously flow inside the capsule to ensure the subject stays asleep. Obviously, this would increase the concentration of the anesthesia-alike gas, and decrease the volume of oxygen over a period of time, making the subject suffocate in their sleep. Therefore, there must always be another personnel nearby to keep track of the oxygen saturation with a gadget such as pulse oximeter. But again, this is no threat towards mankind which is why, I presume, makes it so boring.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One way you can try fixing this is adding some element of danger to the scp. So it can transport people around, but use it too often / too long, and there will be detrimental effects, for example.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>One danger element is as mentioned above, suffocation after a long period of time. The idea of being transported to random locations sounds interesting. The first idea to my concept was a gas mask that, once attached to the user, has same effect as the capsule, but was scratched once I learned about <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-1499">SCP-1499</a> which is basically a gas mask that transports the user to a random location. However, I approve the idea about harmful side effects which I will take into consideration.</p>
<h1><span>Revision</span></h1>
<p>I will revise or go in depth with the following elements:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Threat/danger</strong> - Suffocation, harsh side effects, unpredictable teleportations</li>
<li><strong>SCP-XXXX-1</strong> - Reconsider capabilities (hereby laws of physics and nature)</li>
<li><strong>Writing</strong> - Simpler descriptions</li>
</ul>
<p>Perhaps more points will be added later on, but this is what I assume I should focus on for now.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

