<p>1) You need to keep the old man around a little longer for the sake of the narrative, but cybersqyd touched on that pretty well so I'll basically leave it alone. I'll just mention that it would feel better if he ended up letting the car go on his own, including his loss, rather than relying on The Foundation's devices.</p>
<p>2) The big problem I have is that if the car becomes more destructive the faster it goes but then slows down the more damage it does. However, the car is able to evade capture despite any roadblocks placed in front of it; that doesn't work. if it does significant damage to the roadblock then it should also be slowed down significantly as well, or at least be a far easier target. If the reaction speed also slows down, then this means that the vehicle not only slows down after a collision but also will take longer to get back up to speed.</p>
<p>In short, while it would be able to get past a roadblock, it wouldn't get away from any pursuers, especially if they were ready for it once it got past the roadblock.</p>
<p>3) You may also want to debate the &quot;reduces to atoms&quot; part. Most items used for roadblocks contain a lot of hydrogen and oxygen as part of their make-up; if you reduce items to their atomic components, then that releases all that oxygen and hydrogen to the general area. The fun part here is that oxygen and hydrogen when given a chance combine rather violently into water when given the chance; the explosion is pretty powerful and pretty exothermic.</p>
<p>Now, remember how the vehicle slows down based on how much damage it does? This means that if it does enough damage to slow it down significantly, not only is it going to create a massive explosion, but it's also not going to have the speed necessary to escape the explosion.</p>
<p>You may want to consider stepping it back to a &quot;molecular level&quot; explosion so that it's basically destroyed, but with no nasty side effects.</p>
<p>Interesting idea, but it needs a few tweaks.</p>

