														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4863629">
									

<p>I initially didn't like this SCP - because it conflicts with my headcanon that all the anomalous stuff of the Foundation-verse is <em>natural</em> to the multiverse.</p>
<p>Like, <em>every</em> universe has <em>something</em> a little weird about it - the Foundation-verse is just a place where the barriers between realities have frayed, so there's been a bit of cross-pollination between realities and that's why the Foundation-verse has LOTS of different kinds of weird shit.</p>
<p>But&#8230; how can they be <em>sure</em> there are no anomalies and no Foundation equivalent?</p>
<p>The US government-B says they know nothing about anomalies or a Foundation equivalent. However&#8230; if Foundation-B was doing there jobs properly, the politicians <em>wouldn't</em> know, would they?</p>
<p>I love the idea that there are anomalies and a Foundation - it's just their Foundation is much more effective than ours. Every potentially dangerous SCP (including ones that are barely contained in baseline) was either neutralised or permanently contained, and the few anomalies that are still uncontained are pretty much harmless/unnoticeable.</p>
<p>Because they're more effective, they don't need to reach out for government co-operation and, aside from a few paranoid types in secret service agencies, nobody knows they even <em>exist</em>, let alone what their purpose is.</p>

							</div>

								
					
