<p>Interviews with the creature (it is sapient and capable of speech) and history of its manifestations. It has been around for a long time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So like, this is going to be the bulk of your narrative but like&#8230;what happens here? What story do the interviews tell? What does the history of it's manifestations add?</p>
<p>My concern is you don't really have a cohesive story around this so much as a weird DnD monster; and if you don't have the story down, this won't work.</p>
<hr />
<p>The actual core anomaly feels&#8230;fine but a touch overcomplex perhaps: it has a lot of elements to it that feel a little like they don't necessarily add much; but without having a better idea of what you want to do with the article, I can't tell whether this overcomplexity matters. I guess at it's core my question is: why does this thing bother turning humans into wood in order to burn them in it's lantern instead of like, just using regular wood when it's in a forest? Like obviously narratively, that's more interesting; but the anomaly feels like it needs a motivation for this, y'know?</p>
<p>IMO: just have folk be tempted by a weird song they hear in the woods that makes them go 'wtf is that' and investigate; you don't need to like, making it so that they're forced to be affected by it (at least, that's what I'm assuming the cognitohazard does here)</p>
</div>
